{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KUBm3zCW6MZmMhBaVRwSZA","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd008-ls\n     2→title: \"cmd/ls: Core File Listing\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  ls is the most frequently used Unix utility, and its behavior varies between macOS BSD ls\n     6→  and GNU ls in column layout, color handling, long-format field widths, and human-readable\n     7→  size formatting. Scripts and interactive shells depend on exact output format for parsing\n     8→  and display. A Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gls,\n     9→  installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all in-scope flag combinations, verified by\n    10→  differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n    11→\n    12→  ls is the primary consumer of both pkg/sys and pkg/format. It requires terminal-width\n    13→  queries for multi-column layout, extended file metadata for long format, signal handling\n    14→  for SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH, column alignment for tabular output, ANSI color for file-type\n    15→  colorization, and human-readable size conversion. This PRD covers the core flag subset\n    16→  for rel02.0; extended flags are deferred to prd010-ls-extended.\n    17→\n    18→goals:\n    19→  - G1: List directory contents in multi-column, single-column, or long format, matching GNU ls output layout.\n    20→  - G2: Support filter flags (-a, -A, -d) to control which entries appear in the listing.\n    21→  - G3: Support colorized output via --color with automatic suppression when stdout is not a TTY.\n    22→  - G4: Support human-readable file sizes (-h) in long format, matching GNU ls -lh output.\n    23→  - G5: Produce correct exit codes (0, 1, 2) matching GNU ls exit-code contract.\n    24→\n    25→requirements:\n    26→  R1:\n    27→    title: Default listing (multi-column and single-column)\n    28→    items:\n    29→      - R1.1: |\n    30→          When stdout is a TTY and no format flag is given, must produce multi-column output sorted vertically (down columns, then across). Terminal width is obtained by calling pkg/sys.TerminalWidth() (int, error) — returns the current terminal column count via TIOCGWINSZ ioctl on stdout; returns an error when stdout is not a terminal. Entries are laid out by calling pkg/format.Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string — distributes entries into the maximum number of columns fitting within termWidth, with column widths computed as per-column maxima of entry lengths.\n    31→      - R1.2: When stdout is not a TTY (pipe, file redirect) and no format flag is given, must produce single-column output with one entry per line. This matches GNU ls behavior (ls.c detects stdout is not a TTY and sets format to single-column).\n    32→      - R1.3: Must sort entries in the C locale order (LC_COLLATE=C). Differential tests must set LC_ALL=C to eliminate locale-dependent sort divergence.\n    33→      - R1.4: Must not list entries whose names start with \".\" unless -a or -A is given. This is the default filter behavior matching GNU ls.\n    34→\n    35→  R2:\n    36→    title: Single-column mode (-1) and long format (-l)\n    37→    items:\n    38→      - R2.1: -1 must produce single-column output with one entry per line regardless of whether stdout is a TTY. This overrides the default multi-column layout.\n    39→      - R2a: |\n    40→          -l flag skeleton and permission string. When -l is given, produce long-format output. Field order (left to right): permissions (10 chars), nlink, owner, group, size, mtime, name. Fields separated by a single space. Numeric fields (nlink, size) are right-aligned in columns wide enough for the largest value in the listing.\n    41→\n    42→          Permission string algorithm (10 characters):\n    43→          - Position 0 (file type): 'd' for directory (fi.Mode&os.ModeDir != 0), 'l' for symlink (fi.Mode&os.ModeSymlink != 0), 'c' for char device (fi.Mode&os.ModeDevice != 0 && fi.Mode&os.ModeCharDevice != 0), 'b' for block device (fi.Mode&os.ModeDevice != 0 && fi.Mode&os.ModeCharDevice == 0), 'p' for FIFO (fi.Mode&os.ModeNamedPipe != 0), 's' for socket (fi.Mode&os.ModeSocket != 0), '-' for regular file.\n    44→          - Positions 1-3 (owner rwx): 'r'/'- ' for bit 0o400, 'w'/'-' for 0o200, 'x'/'-' for 0o100. If setuid (fi.Mode&os.ModeSetuid != 0): replace 'x' with 's', or replace '-' with 'S'.\n    45→          - Positions 4-6 (group rwx): bits 0o040, 0o020, 0o010. If setgid (fi.Mode&os.ModeSetgid != 0): replace 'x' with 's', or replace '-' with 'S'.\n    46→          - Positions 7-9 (other rwx): bits 0o004, 0o002, 0o001. If sticky (fi.Mode&os.ModeSticky != 0): replace 'x' with 't', or replace '-' with 'T'.\n    47→      - R2b: |\n    48→          File metadata via pkg/sys.Lstat. Obtain metadata for each listed entry by calling pkg/sys.Lstat(path string) (*sys.FileInfo, error) — does not follow symbolic links; returns the symlink's own metadata when path is a symlink.\n    49→\n    50→          sys.FileInfo struct (embedded here for reference):\n    51→            type FileInfo struct {\n    52→                Mode    os.FileMode // file mode and type bits\n    53→                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n    54→                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n    55→                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n    56→                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n    57→                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n    58→                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n    59→                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n    60→                Blocks  int64       // number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n    61→                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size (st_blksize)\n    62→                ModTime time.Time   // modification time\n    63→                Sys     os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo\n    64→            }\n    65→\n    66→          In long format: print fi.Nlink (uint64) right-aligned; print fi.Size (int64, bytes) right-aligned. Both columns sized to the widest value across all listed entries.\n    67→      - R2c: |\n    68→          Owner and group name resolution in long format. Resolve owner name by calling os/user.LookupId(strconv.Itoa(int(fi.Uid))); on success use u.Username; on error (user not found or lookup fails) fall back to strconv.FormatUint(uint64(fi.Uid), 10). Resolve group name by calling os/user.LookupGroupId(strconv.Itoa(int(fi.Gid))); on success use g.Name; on error fall back to strconv.FormatUint(uint64(fi.Gid), 10). Owner and group fields are left-padded to column width for alignment.\n    69→      - R2d: |\n    70→          Modification time formatting in long format. Use fi.ModTime (time.Time from sys.FileInfo). If time.Since(fi.ModTime) < 6*30*24*time.Hour (approximately six months), format as fi.ModTime.Format(\"Jan _2 15:04\"). Otherwise format as fi.ModTime.Format(\"Jan _2  2006\"). Both patterns produce a 12-character field.\n    71→      - R2e: |\n    72→          Total block count line and symlink display. Before the directory listing, print \"total N\\n\" where N = sum(fi.Blocks for all listed entries) / 2. The division converts 512-byte st_blocks units to 1K-block units matching GNU ls. N is a plain integer (no trailing suffix) unless -h is active (see R5).\n    73→\n    74→          Symlink display: when fi.Mode&os.ModeSymlink != 0, read the link target with os.Readlink(path) and append \" -> \" + target to the entry name field. Use Lstat (not Stat) so the symlink's own fi.Size and fi.Nlink are used, not the target's.\n    75→\n    76→  R3:\n    77→    title: Filter flags (-a, -A, -d)\n    78→    items:\n    79→      - R3.1: -a must include entries whose names start with \".\" including the special entries \".\" and \"..\" in the listing.\n    80→      - R3.2: -A must include entries whose names start with \".\" except for \".\" and \"..\". -A is the \"almost all\" filter.\n    81→      - R3.3: -d must list directory entries themselves rather than their contents. When a directory is given as an argument with -d, must print the directory name (or its long-format entry with -l) without descending into it.\n    82→      - R3.4: When both -a and -A are given, the last one on the command line takes precedence, matching GNU ls behavior.\n    83→\n    84→  R4:\n    85→    title: Color output (--color)\n    86→    items:\n    87→      - R4.1: Must support --color=always, --color=auto, and --color=never. When --color is given without a value, it must default to \"always\".\n    88→      - R4.2: |\n    89→          --color=auto must colorize output only when stdout is a TTY. TTY detection: call pkg/sys.IsTerminal(os.Stdout) — returns true when os.Stdout's file descriptor is a terminal (detected via golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize with TIOCGWINSZ), false otherwise. When stdout is not a TTY, --color=auto must behave like --color=never.\n    90→      - R4.3: |\n    91→          Colorized output uses GNU LS_COLORS default color assignments via pkg/format. Call pkg/format.FileTypeColor(mode os.FileMode) string to obtain the ANSI escape sequence for a given file type, and pkg/format.Reset() string for the reset sequence. Executable detection: fi.Mode&0o111 != 0 (any execute bit set). Each entry name is wrapped as: colorCode + name + resetCode. When color is suppressed, both codes are empty strings so no ANSI sequences appear in output.\n    92→\n    93→          Set the process-global color mode based on the --color flag value: call pkg/format.SetColorEnabled(true) for --color=always, pkg/format.SetColorEnabled(false) for --color=never. For --color=auto, call pkg/sys.IsTerminal(os.Stdout.Fd()) and pass the result to pkg/format.SetColorEnabled. When color is disabled, pkg/format.FileTypeColor returns empty strings.\n    94→      - R4.4: When --color=never or when color is suppressed (non-TTY with auto), must not emit any ANSI escape sequences in the output.\n    95→\n    96→  R5:\n    97→    title: Human-readable sizes (-h)\n    98→    items:\n    99→      - R5.1: |\n   100→          -h must only take effect with -l (long format). When -h is given with -l, file sizes (fi.Size int64) must be displayed via pkg/format.HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string with opts.Binary = true (1024-base units, suffixes K/M/G/T/P/E). Without -l, -h must have no visible effect on output."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012K74d4uRySevU3JpsUv6vV","session_id":"b3986247-14f1-40ab-8591-8e0beb64d116","uuid":"1eda6248-3dac-4b66-b48b-dee0bcbb403e"}
