{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011Ho9hSZZw3ymcmDCkEgjUS","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd010-ls-extended\n     2→title: \"cmd/ls: Extended Flags\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  prd008-ls covers the core flag subset for ls (default listing, -1, -l, -a, -A, -d, --color,\n     6→  -h). The extended flags add multi-column layout control (-C, -x), sort order options (-t,\n     7→  -S, -r, -U, -v), metadata display (-i, -s, -n), file type classification (-F), and\n     8→  recursive listing (-R). These flags require heavier interaction with pkg/format.Columns\n     9→  for layout control, pkg/sys.Stat for sort-by-metadata and inode/block display, and more\n    10→  complex output normalization in differential tests (inode numbers, block counts, and\n    11→  modification times all vary across test runs).\n    12→\n    13→  We extend prd008-ls here. All requirements in prd008-ls remain in force; we add the\n    14→  extended flag behavior without repeating core requirements.\n    15→\n    16→goals:\n    17→  - G1: Support multi-column layout control flags (-C, -x) for forced columnar output with vertical or horizontal sorting.\n    18→  - G2: Support sort order flags (-t, -S, -r, -U, -v) to control entry ordering by time, size, version, or directory order.\n    19→  - G3: Support metadata display flags (-i, -s, -n) for inode numbers, block counts, and numeric user/group IDs.\n    20→  - G4: Support file type classification (-F) to append type indicators to entry names.\n    21→  - G5: Support recursive directory listing (-R) to list subdirectory contents.\n    22→\n    23→requirements:\n    24→  R1:\n    25→    title: Multi-column layout (-C, -x)\n    26→    items:\n    27→      - R1.1: |\n    28→          -C must force multi-column output regardless of whether stdout is a TTY. Layout uses pkg/format.Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string — distributes entries into the maximum number of columns fitting within termWidth, with column widths computed as per-column maxima. Terminal width is obtained via pkg/sys.TerminalWidth() (int, error) — returns current terminal column count via TIOCGWINSZ ioctl; returns error when stdout is not a terminal. When stdout is not a TTY, -C must use a default width of 80 columns (matching GNU ls behavior when stdout is not a TTY and -C is given without -w).\n    29→      - R1.2: -C must sort entries vertically (down columns, then across to the next column). This is the same sort direction as the default multi-column mode in prd008-ls R1.1.\n    30→      - R1.3: -x must produce multi-column output with entries sorted horizontally (across columns, then down to the next row). This differs from -C which sorts vertically.\n    31→      - R1.4: -C and -x are mutually exclusive with -l and -1. If -l or -1 appears after -C or -x on the command line, it overrides the multi-column mode. If -C or -x appears after -l or -1, it overrides the long or single-column format. The last format flag wins, matching GNU ls behavior.\n    32→\n    33→  R2:\n    34→    title: Sort order (-t, -S, -r, -U, -v)\n    35→    items:\n    36→      - R2.1: |\n    37→          -t must sort entries by modification time, newest first. Obtain metadata via pkg/sys.Lstat(path string) (*sys.FileInfo, error); use sys.FileInfo.ModTime time.Time for comparison (fi.ModTime.After(fj.ModTime) for newest-first ordering). Entries with the same modification time must be sorted by name in C locale order as a tiebreaker.\n    38→      - R2.2: |\n    39→          -S must sort entries by file size (st_size), largest first. Obtain file size from sys.FileInfo.Size int64 — apparent file size in bytes (st_size). Entries with the same size must be sorted by name in C locale order as a tiebreaker.\n    40→      - R2.3: -r must reverse the current sort order. -r applies to whichever sort is active (name sort by default, -t for time, -S for size, -v for version). -r with the default sort produces reverse alphabetical order.\n    41→      - R2.4: -U must disable sorting entirely and list entries in directory order (the order returned by readdir). -U overrides -t, -S, and -v. -r with -U has no defined effect (directory order is not reversible in a meaningful way), but must be accepted without error.\n    42→      - R2.5: -v must sort entries using natural version sort semantics (strverscmp behavior). Runs of digits within filenames are compared numerically rather than lexicographically, so \"file2\" sorts before \"file10\". Must be implemented without external libraries.\n    43→      - R2.6: When multiple sort flags are given, the last one on the command line takes precedence, matching GNU ls behavior.\n    44→\n    45→  R3:\n    46→    title: Metadata display (-i, -s, -n)\n    47→    items:\n    48→      - R3.1: |\n    49→          -i must prepend the inode number of each entry before the entry name (or before the permissions in -l mode). Obtain the inode number via pkg/sys.Lstat(path string) (*sys.FileInfo, error) and read sys.FileInfo.Ino uint64 — inode number (st_ino). The inode number is right-aligned in a column wide enough for the largest inode in the listing.\n    50→      - R3.2: |\n    51→          -s must prepend the allocated block count for each entry before the entry name (or before the permissions in -l mode). Obtain the block count via pkg/sys.Lstat(path string) (*sys.FileInfo, error) and read sys.FileInfo.Blocks int64 — number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks). Report in 1024-byte block units: fi.Blocks / 2. The block count is right-aligned in a column wide enough for the largest count in the listing.\n    52→      - R3.3: -s with -l must also modify the \"total N\" line to include block counts in the selected unit. The total is the sum of all listed entries' block allocations.\n    53→      - R3.4: |\n    54→          -n must activate long format (implies -l) and display numeric UID and GID instead of resolving owner and group names. Format UID as strconv.FormatUint(uint64(fi.Uid), 10) and GID as strconv.FormatUint(uint64(fi.Gid), 10), where fi.Uid uint32 and fi.Gid uint32 come from pkg/sys.Lstat. Do not call os/user.LookupId or os/user.LookupGroupId.\n    55→      - R3.5: -i and -s may be combined. When both are given, inode is printed first, then block count, then the entry (or the long-format fields).\n    56→\n    57→  R4:\n    58→    title: File type classification (-F)\n    59→    items:\n    60→      - R4.1: -F must append a type indicator character after each entry name. The indicators are \"/\" for directories, \"*\" for executable regular files, \"@\" for symbolic links, \"|\" for named pipes (FIFOs), \"=\" for sockets. Regular non-executable files receive no indicator.\n    61→      - R4.2: Executable is defined as having any execute bit set (owner, group, or other) in the file's permission mode.\n    62→      - R4.3: -F must work with all output formats (-l, -1, -C, -x) and with color output. When color is enabled, the indicator character is printed after the ANSI reset sequence so it is not colorized.\n    63→\n    64→  R5:\n    65→    title: Recursive listing (-R)\n    66→    items:\n    67→      - R5.1: -R must recursively list the contents of each subdirectory encountered during the listing. Each subdirectory's contents are printed under a header line in the format \"PATH:\" followed by a blank line, then the directory contents, then a blank line before the next entry.\n    68→      - R5.2: -R must respect the current format mode. With -l, each subdirectory's contents are listed in long format including the \"total N\" block line. With -C or default mode, each subdirectory's contents use multi-column layout.\n    69→      - R5.3: -R must not follow symbolic links to directories. Only real subdirectories are recursed into.\n    70→      - R5.4: -R must apply the current filter flags (-a, -A) to each subdirectory. Dotfiles in subdirectories are shown only if -a or -A is given.\n    71→      - R5.5: -R must list directories in the same sort order as entries within a directory. If -t is active, subdirectories are recursed into in modification-time order.\n    72→\n    73→  R6:\n    74→    title: Flag interactions\n    75→    items:\n    76→      - R6.1: -n implies -l. When -n is given without -l, the output must be in long format with numeric UID/GID.\n    77→      - R6.2: -C and -x are mutually exclusive with -l and -1. The last format flag on the command line wins (see R1.4).\n    78→      - R6.3: -R with -l must produce a \"total N\" block line for each subdirectory listing, matching GNU ls -lR output.\n    79→      - R6.4: -i, -s, and -F may all be combined with -R. Each subdirectory listing applies the same metadata display and classification options.\n    80→      - R6.5: |\n    81→          -h must apply to -s block counts when both -s and -h are given, converting block counts to human-readable form. Use the following binary HumanSize algorithm directly in cmd/ls (do not call pkg/format.HumanSize):\n    82→\n    83→            func humanSize(n int64) string {\n    84→                if n == 0 { return \"0\" }\n    85→                suffixes := []string{\"\", \"K\", \"M\", \"G\", \"T\", \"P\", \"E\"}\n    86→                f := float64(n)\n    87→                i := 0\n    88→                for f >= 1024 && i < len(suffixes)-1 { f /= 1024; i++ }\n    89→                if i == 0 { return fmt.Sprintf(\"%d\", n) }\n    90→                if f == float64(int64(f)) { return fmt.Sprintf(\"%d%s\", int64(f), suffixes[i]) }\n    91→                return fmt.Sprintf(\"%.1f%s\", f, suffixes[i])\n    92→            }\n    93→\n    94→non_goals:\n    95→  - --hyperlink (terminal hyperlinks) remains out of scope for this project.\n    96→  - --dired (Emacs dired mode) remains out of scope.\n    97→  - -Z (SELinux security context) remains out of scope.\n    98→  - --quoting-style (filename quoting) remains out of scope.\n    99→  - --block-size=SIZE with arbitrary unit suffixes remains out of scope.\n   100→  - LS_COLORS environment variable parsing remains out of scope.\n   101→  - ACL display in long format (the \"+\" indicator) remains out of scope.\n   102→  - --group-directories-first is out of scope for this release.\n   103→  - -w / --width (explicit width override) is out of scope; terminal width is queried via pkg/sys.\n   104→  - --sort=WORD (long-form sort option) is out of scope; only short flags (-t, -S, -r, -U, -v) are supported.\n   105→\n   106→acceptance_criteria:\n   107→  - \"AC1: Multi-column forced -- gls -C and go run ./cmd/ls -C produce identical column layout on a test fixture when both are given the same terminal width. Output must be piped (stdout not a TTY) to verify that -C forces multi-column even without a TTY.\"\n   108→  - \"AC2: Horizontal sort -- gls -x and go run ./cmd/ls -x produce identical output on a fixture. Entries must be arranged across rows first, then down.\"\n   109→  - \"AC3: Time sort -- gls -t and go run ./cmd/ls -t produce identical output on a fixture with files of known different modification times. Tests must create files with explicit mtimes to ensure deterministic ordering.\"\n   110→  - \"AC4: Size sort -- gls -S and go run ./cmd/ls -S produce identical output on a fixture with files of known different sizes.\"\n   111→  - \"AC5: Reverse sort -- gls -r and go run ./cmd/ls -r produce identical reverse-alphabetical output. gls -tr and the Go binary with -tr produce identical reverse-time output.\"\n   112→  - \"AC6: Inode display -- gls -i and go run ./cmd/ls -i produce identical output when run on the same live fixture on the same filesystem (inode numbers match because both binaries stat the same files).\"\n   113→  - \"AC7: Block count display -- gls -s and go run ./cmd/ls -s produce identical output. Differential tests must use the same filesystem to ensure block counts match.\"\n   114→  - \"AC8: Numeric IDs -- gls -n and go run ./cmd/ls -n produce identical long-format output with numeric UID and GID instead of user/group names.\"\n   115→  - \"AC9: Classify -- gls -F and go run ./cmd/ls -F produce identical output on a fixture containing a directory, a regular file, an executable, a symlink, and a named pipe.\"\n   116→  - \"AC10: Recursive listing -- gls -R and go run ./cmd/ls -R produce identical output on a fixture with nested subdirectories, including the \\\"path:\\\" headers and blank-line separators.\"\n   117→  - \"AC11: Version sort -- gls -v and go run ./cmd/ls -v produce identical output on a fixture containing files named file1, file2, file10, file20 (verifying numeric-aware sort).\"\n   118→  - \"AC12: cmd/ls with extended flags compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n   119→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012K74d4uRySevU3JpsUv6vV","session_id":"b3986247-14f1-40ab-8591-8e0beb64d116","uuid":"a43a460c-f128-4ef4-bd62-d961e9a14f47"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_018RM5GmFA33dUotwy9fgx5u","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LiY2YLLTeLJLchSYLN1ZUZ","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/generation-2026-02-27-21-48-32-21v/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd002-sys.yaml","limit":150},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3861,"cache_read_input_tokens":27759,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3861},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012K74d4uRySevU3JpsUv6vV","session_id":"b3986247-14f1-40ab-8591-8e0beb64d116","uuid":"5d78dff8-5cd6-483b-96ad-95ecde23a56a"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LiY2YLLTeLJLchSYLN1ZUZ","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd002-sys\n     2→title: \"pkg/sys: Syscall Abstractions\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  Four utilities (ls, du, find, xargs) need OS-level capabilities that Go's standard\n     6→  library does not expose in a portable, stable form: terminal-width queries, extended\n     7→  file metadata (hard-link count, device and inode numbers, physical block counts),\n     8→  and signal handling for SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH. On Darwin and Linux these syscalls\n     9→  diverge in struct layout (st_mtimespec vs st_mtim, st_blocks interpretation) and\n    10→  available interfaces (TIOCGWINSZ ioctl). Without pkg/sys each cmd/ package would\n    11→  import syscall or golang.org/x/sys directly, duplicating the Darwin/Linux divergence\n    12→  handling and making it harder to test or audit the syscall surface.\n    13→\n    14→  pkg/sys provides a stable, tested interface for these three capability areas. Each\n    15→  capability is driven by at least one concrete utility requirement derived from C source\n    16→  analysis (see dependency-map.yaml).\n    17→\n    18→goals:\n    19→  - G1: Provide a portable terminal-width query that works on Darwin and Linux without cgo.\n    20→  - G2: Expose extended file metadata (st_nlink, st_uid, st_gid, st_rdev, st_blocks, st_ino, st_dev) not available from os.FileInfo.\n    21→  - G3: Provide a SIGPIPE handler that suppresses the broken-pipe error when stdout is closed by a downstream consumer.\n    22→  - G4: Provide a SIGWINCH handler that calls a user-supplied callback when the terminal is resized.\n    23→\n    24→requirements:\n    25→  R1:\n    26→    title: Terminal width and TTY detection\n    27→    items:\n    28→      - R1.1: Must provide func TerminalWidth() (int, error) that returns the current terminal column count by calling ioctl(TIOCGWINSZ) on stdout. Returns an error (not panic) when stdout is not a terminal or when ioctl fails.\n    29→      - R1.2: Must use golang.org/x/sys/unix (the project's only external dependency) to perform the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl; must not shell out to tput or stty and must not import golang.org/x/term.\n    30→      - R1.3: |\n    31→          Must provide func IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool that returns true when the file\n    32→          descriptor refers to a terminal, and false otherwise. Implementation: call\n    33→          golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize(int(fd), unix.TIOCGWINSZ) and return\n    34→          err == nil. Returns false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors.\n    35→          The caller (pkg/format ColorEnabled) is responsible for extracting the fd from an\n    36→          io.Writer via type assertion to *os.File. Required by pkg/format to suppress color\n    37→          output when stdout is redirected.\n    38→      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281): ls uses struct winsize to determine column count for its default multi-column layout and for the -w/--width flag default.'\n    39→\n    40→  R2:\n    41→    title: Extended file metadata\n    42→    items:\n    43→      - R2.1: Must provide func Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) and func Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error). Stat follows symbolic links (equivalent to os.Stat); Lstat does not follow symbolic links (equivalent to os.Lstat). Both return a *FileInfo populated from syscall.Stat_t.\n    44→      - R2.2: |\n    45→          FileInfo must be defined as the following named struct in package sys:\n    46→\n    47→            type FileInfo struct {\n    48→                Mode    os.FileMode // file mode and type bits (from syscall.Stat_t.Mode)\n    49→                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n    50→                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n    51→                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n    52→                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n    53→                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n    54→                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n    55→                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n    56→                Blocks  int64       // number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n    57→                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size (st_blksize)\n    58→                ModTime time.Time   // modification time (st_mtime / st_mtimespec)\n    59→                Info    os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo for os package compatibility\n    60→            }\n    61→      - R2.3: Must abstract the Darwin/Linux divergence in struct field names (st_mtimespec on Darwin vs st_mtim on Linux; st_blocks interpretation) so callers use a single API regardless of target platform.\n    62→      - R2.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:213, 371): ls needs Nlink, Uid, Gid, Rdev, Mode for -l format; Blocks for -s; Ino for -i.'\n    63→      - R2.5: 'Utility context — du (du.c:554): du needs Dev and Ino for hard-link deduplication, and Blocks for physical block-count accumulation.'\n    64→      - R2.6: 'Utility context — find (find/ftsfind.c:172): find predicates (-type, -size, -newer, -perm, -uid, -gid) require Mode, Size, ModTime, Uid, Gid, Nlink from the stat struct.'\n    65→\n    66→  R3:\n    67→    title: SIGPIPE handling\n    68→    items:\n    69→      - R3.1: |\n    70→          Must provide func InstallSIGPIPEHandler() that installs a SIGPIPE handler causing the process to exit 0 when stdout or stderr is closed by a downstream consumer. Implementation: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Must not use signal.Ignore so that deferred cleanup functions do not run on SIGPIPE (matching GNU behavior where SIGPIPE terminates silently).\n    71→      - R3.2: InstallSIGPIPEHandler must be safe to call multiple times; only one goroutine per invocation is started.\n    72→      - R3.3: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:1600-1620): ls installs a SIGPIPE handler via sigaction so that piping ls output to head does not produce a broken-pipe error message. du, find, and xargs have the same requirement.'\n    73→\n    74→  R4:\n    75→    title: SIGWINCH handling\n    76→    items:\n    77→      - R4.1: |\n    78→          Must provide func OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that registers a SIGWINCH handler. When the terminal is resized, the handler calls TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width. If TerminalWidth() returns an error the callback is not invoked.\n    79→      - R4.2: Must support multiple calls to OnTerminalResize; each registered callback must be called on resize. Callbacks are called in registration order.\n    80→      - R4.3: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281, SIGWINCH registration): ls re-queries terminal width on SIGWINCH to re-format output when the terminal window is resized during a long listing.'\n    81→\n    82→non_goals:\n    83→  - pkg/sys does not wrap all of syscall or golang.org/x/sys; it exposes only the surfaces required by the current utility batch.\n    84→  - pkg/sys does not provide file-watching or inotify/kqueue interfaces.\n    85→  - pkg/sys does not handle SIGUSR1/SIGUSR2 (find's debug output); those are deferred to the find PRD.\n    86→  - pkg/sys does not provide process-group or terminal control (TIOCGPGRP, tcsetpgrp); those are deferred to the xargs PRD.\n    87→\n    88→acceptance_criteria:\n    89→  - \"AC1: TerminalWidth() returns a positive integer when called in a test with a pseudo-terminal (golang.org/x/term's test helpers or os.Pipe() as a TTY substitute is acceptable).\"\n    90→  - \"AC2: Stat() and Lstat() on a known file return FileInfo with correct Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino, and Blocks values as confirmed by running stat(1) on the same file.\"\n    91→  - \"AC3: Lstat() on a symlink returns the symlink's own st_mode (type bits show symlink), not the target's mode.\"\n    92→  - \"AC4: InstallSIGPIPEHandler() causes a process writing to a broken pipe to exit 0 rather than printing a write error.\"\n    93→  - \"AC5: All pkg/sys code compiles on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64 with no build errors and no cgo.\"\n    94→  - \"AC6: All pkg/sys code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    95→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012K74d4uRySevU3JpsUv6vV","session_id":"b3986247-14f1-40ab-8591-8e0beb64d116","uuid":"f400d734-5a78-4a98-afe1-331f1436d28c"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_017sPZbqPG4RsMcBE4jdxAJG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015PY542MAWLz9aS5HYYfTko","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/generation-2026-02-27-21-48-32-21v/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd003-format.yaml","limit":150},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2674,"cache_read_input_tokens":31620,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2674},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012K74d4uRySevU3JpsUv6vV","session_id":"b3986247-14f1-40ab-8591-8e0beb64d116","uuid":"0333bc71-87a7-49bc-94ca-0e411c60a2e8"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015PY542MAWLz9aS5HYYfTko","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd003-format\n     2→title: \"pkg/format: Output Formatting\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  Both ls and du produce human-readable output that requires formatting logic beyond\n     6→  fmt.Sprintf: ls produces column-aligned tabular output with ANSI color codes for\n     7→  file types, and both ls and du convert raw byte counts to human-readable sizes\n     8→  (4.0K, 128M, 1.5G). Without pkg/format each cmd/ package would implement its own\n     9→  column width computation, ANSI escape handling, and unit conversion, producing\n    10→  inconsistent behavior across utilities and making differential test normalization\n    11→  harder.\n    12→\n    13→  The formatting logic is driven by C source requirements in ls.c and du.c. Each\n    14→  capability is listed with its utility context so the API reflects real call sites,\n    15→  not speculation.\n    16→\n    17→goals:\n    18→  - G1: Provide a column-width computation and padding function for multi-column tabular output, matching ls column-layout behavior.\n    19→  - G2: Provide an ANSI color lookup and output function for file-type colorization, with automatic suppression when stdout is not a TTY.\n    20→  - G3: Provide a human-readable size conversion function matching GNU coreutils human_readable() behavior for both ls -h and du -h.\n    21→\n    22→requirements:\n    23→  R1:\n    24→    title: Column alignment\n    25→    items:\n    26→      - R1.1: Must provide a Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string function that distributes entries into the maximum number of columns that fit within termWidth, using the longest entry in each column to determine column width.\n    27→      - R1.2: Must provide a PadRight(s string, width int) string and PadLeft(s string, width int) string function for left- and right-aligned field padding within a fixed-width column.\n    28→      - R1.3: 'Must treat multi-byte Unicode characters correctly: column width must be computed from display width (rune count via utf8.RuneCountInString), not byte count. East-asian full-width character handling is deferred; rune count is sufficient for ASCII-dominant utility output in the current roadmap.'\n    29→      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c multi-column layout): ls default output places filenames in as many columns as fit in the terminal width. Column widths are computed from the longest filename in each column, not the global maximum.'\n    30→\n    31→  R2:\n    32→    title: ANSI color\n    33→    items:\n    34→      - R2.1: Must provide a FileTypeColor(mode os.FileMode) string function that returns the ANSI escape sequence for a file's type (directory, symlink, executable, block device, character device, socket, pipe, regular file).\n    35→      - R2.2: Must provide a Reset() string constant or function that returns the ANSI reset sequence (\"\\033[0m\").\n    36→      - R2.3: |\n    37→          Must provide a ColorEnabled(w io.Writer) bool function that returns true only when w is a TTY. Implementation: attempt a type assertion to *os.File; if it fails return false; call pkg/sys.IsTerminal(f.Fd()) which returns true when the file descriptor is a terminal (detected via TIOCGWINSZ ioctl), and false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors. When ColorEnabled returns false, FileTypeColor must return an empty string and Reset must return an empty string.\n    38→      - R2.4: 'Must use the same color assignments as GNU ls LS_COLORS defaults: directory=34 (blue), symlink=36 (cyan), executable=32 (green), block device=33;1 (yellow bold), character device=33;1 (yellow bold), socket=35 (magenta), pipe=33 (yellow), regular=0 (reset/default).'\n    39→      - R2.5: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:103 includes dircolors.h, ls.c:273 get_color_indicator): ls outputs ANSI escapes before and after each filename in colorized mode. Color is suppressed when stdout is not a TTY or when --color=never is set.'\n    40→      - R2.6: |\n    41→          Must provide func SetColorEnabled(enabled bool) that overrides the automatic TTY\n    42→          detection from ColorEnabled. When set to true, FileTypeColor and Reset return ANSI\n    43→          sequences regardless of whether stdout is a TTY. When set to false, they return\n    44→          empty strings regardless. This supports --color=always and --color=never flags in\n    45→          utilities like ls. The override is process-global.\n    46→      - R2.7: |\n    47→          Must provide func ResetColorEnabled() that clears any override set by\n    48→          SetColorEnabled, reverting to automatic TTY detection via ColorEnabled. Utilities\n    49→          must call ResetColorEnabled in deferred cleanup or test teardown to avoid leaking\n    50→          the override across test cases.\n    51→\n    52→  R3:\n    53→    title: Human-readable size conversion\n    54→    items:\n    55→      - R3.1: |\n    56→          Must provide func HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string that converts a byte count to a human-readable string. HumanSizeOpts is defined as:\n    57→\n    58→            type HumanSizeOpts struct {\n    59→                Binary bool // true = 1024-based with suffixes K/M/G/T/P/E (default); false = SI 1000-based with suffixes kB/MB/GB/TB\n    60→            }\n    61→      - R3.2: |\n    62→          When opts.Binary is true (the default zero value), use base 1024 and suffixes []string{\"\", \"K\", \"M\", \"G\", \"T\", \"P\", \"E\"}. When opts.Binary is false, use base 1000 and suffixes []string{\"\", \"kB\", \"MB\", \"GB\", \"TB\"}.\n    63→      - R3.3: Must format the output to at most one decimal place when the value is not an integer at the chosen unit (e.g., 1536 bytes must produce \"1.5K\", not \"2K\").\n    64→      - R3.4: Must return \"0\" for a zero byte count regardless of the unit mode.\n    65→      - R3.5: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c -h flag, human_output_opts): ls -h shows file sizes in human-readable form in -l output. The same conversion applies to ls -s (block counts).'\n    66→      - R3.6: 'Utility context — du (du.c:153 human_output_opts, du.c:442 human_readable()): du -h outputs directory sizes as human-readable strings. du uses the same binary/SI distinction as ls -h.'\n    67→\n    68→non_goals:\n    69→  - pkg/format does not read or parse LS_COLORS environment variables; it uses GNU ls defaults only.\n    70→  - pkg/format does not implement terminal hyperlinks (ls.c:210 mentions them as a feature but they are not in scope).\n    71→  - pkg/format does not provide a full table library; it provides only the column-alignment primitives needed by ls and du.\n    72→  - pkg/format does not handle color themes beyond the GNU ls defaults.\n    73→\n    74→acceptance_criteria:\n    75→  - \"AC1: Columns(entries, termWidth) with a list of 20 filenames and termWidth=80 produces the same column layout as gls --format=across for the same input.\"\n    76→  - \"AC2: FileTypeColor returns the correct ANSI code for each of the eight file types listed in R2.4.\"\n    77→  - \"AC3: ColorEnabled returns false when passed an io.Writer backed by an os.Pipe (not a TTY), and true when passed os.Stdout in a TTY test.\"\n    78→  - \"AC4: HumanSize(1536, binary) returns \\\"1.5K\\\". HumanSize(1000000, SI) returns \\\"1.0MB\\\". HumanSize(0, binary) returns \\\"0\\\".\"\n    79→  - \"AC5: SetColorEnabled(true) causes FileTypeColor to return ANSI codes even when stdout is a pipe. SetColorEnabled(false) causes FileTypeColor to return empty strings even when stdout is a TTY. ResetColorEnabled reverts to automatic detection.\"\n    80→  - \"AC6: All pkg/format code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    81→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012K74d4uRySevU3JpsUv6vV","session_id":"b3986247-14f1-40ab-8591-8e0beb64d116","uuid":"8d641cb7-58bf-45d0-b35f-182effedd88b"}
