{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01KJEY8q18LHdS6CNSEpUHjq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The output looks good but I notice one issue with the -nF combined test. For the symlink entry `link.txt -> regular.txt@`, the `@` indicator is appended after the target. But GNU ls puts the indicator after the name only, not after the symlink target. Let me check: in long format, does GNU ls append the classify indicator to the symlink entry?\n\nActually, looking at GNU ls behavior with `-lF`:\n- For symlinks in long format, GNU ls shows `link.txt -> target` with `@` indicator. But where does the `@` go?\n\nLet me think about this. In GNU ls -lF:\n\n\nThe long format output shows the symlink without the `@` indicator since the `l` in the permissions already identifies it as a symlink, but in non-long format with `-F`, the `@` gets appended to the name. I need to verify whether GNU ls actually adds the classification indicator in both formats or just the short format. I'm uncertain whether GNU ls actually appends the `@` indicator after symlinks in long format, or if it's omitted since the `l` prefix already signals it's a symlink. Let me check the source code to see exactly how the classification indicator gets printed relative to the target information. I need to verify the actual GNU ls behavior since I can't test it directly here. The PRD says -F appends a type indicator after the entry name, but I'm uncertain whether that applies to the filename alone or the full display string including the symlink target. Looking at typical GNU ls output, symlinks in long format don't actually show the @ indicator—it's just the filename followed by the arrow and target. I should implement what the PRD specifies and let the differential tests reveal any discrepancies. I need to verify exactly how GNU ls handles the `@` indicator in long format with symlinks—whether it appears before or after the arrow notatio